## THEORETICAL FRAME — EIM & IS Regulation Theory

### Tallon, P. P., Ramirez, R. V., & Short, J. E. (2013)

**Full citation:** Tallon, P. P., Ramirez, R. V., & Short, J. E. (2013). The information artifact in IT governance: Toward a theory of information governance. *Journal of Management Information Systems*, *30*(3), 141–178.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — EIM parent theory  
**Claim:** Enterprise information management requires governance structures that align information assets with organisational strategy — the information artefact is the unit of governance, not the technology infrastructure.  
**Where used:** §2.1 (EIM theoretical foundation; establishes information governance as organisational capability)

---

### Ross, J. W., Weill, P., & Robertson, D. C. (2006)

**Full citation:** Ross, J. W., Weill, P., & Robertson, D. C. (2006). *Enterprise architecture as strategy: Creating a foundation for business execution*. Harvard Business School Press.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — enterprise architecture and governance  
**Claim:** Enterprise architecture provides the foundation through which governance frameworks allocate decision rights and accountability for information assets across organisational boundaries.  
**Where used:** §2.1 (EIM governance structures; supports the argument that mandate architecture reshapes enterprise information architecture)

